course: everyday
lesson: 258
title: The lunchroom and school trips
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many schools are nut-free because even a tiny trace of nut can harm a child with a severe allergy, so you do not bring nuts or peanut butter when the school asks you not to.
explain: In the lunch hall you try a little of new food, tell a supervisor about spills so nobody slips, sort rubbish into the right bins, and quietly tell a teacher if a classmate has no lunch, so the school can help without embarrassing them.
explain: On a trip you stay with your buddy all day, so nobody can get lost without someone noticing; in a museum you walk, talk quietly and do not touch exhibits unless a sign allows it.
explain: Farm animals can carry germs such as E. coli, so you touch them only with permission and wash your hands well afterwards.
explain: A boy from Glasgow on a school camp feels homesick on the first night; he tells a teacher, and by the next afternoon, busy with canoeing, he feels much better.
fact: Many schools are nut-free, because tiny traces of nut can harm a child with a severe allergy.
fact: If you do not like the school lunch, you try a little and politely leave the rest, or talk to your parents about a packed lunch.
fact: If you forget your lunch, you tell the teacher or the office, who can phone home or give you something to eat.
fact: If you spill a drink, you tell a lunch supervisor and help clean it up, because wet floors make people slip.
fact: Rubbish goes in the right bin: recycling, compost or general waste.
fact: If a classmate has no lunch, you tell a teacher quietly; many schools offer free school meals.
fact: On a trip you stay with your buddy all day and tell the teacher if you get separated.
fact: If you feel travel sick, you tell the teacher early, sit near the front and look out at the horizon (the far line where the land or sea meets the sky).
fact: In a museum or gallery you walk, talk quietly and touch exhibits only where a sign allows it, because hands can damage precious old things.
fact: On a farm you touch animals only with permission and wash your hands well afterwards, because they can carry germs such as E. coli.
fact: Before a trip you decide how much money to spend on souvenirs and keep it safe.
fact: On a residential trip (one where you stay away overnight) you label your things, and feeling homesick is normal and usually passes quickly.
exercise: Your school is nut-free, and you love peanut butter sandwiches. What do you do? | Bring a different sandwich to school | Bring it but eat it quickly | Bring it and share it carefully | Bring it hidden in your bag
exercise: Why can a tiny bit of peanut be dangerous at school? | It can harm a child with a severe allergy | Peanuts go bad very quickly in a warm classroom | Peanuts make the floor slippery | It attracts wasps to the school
exercise: You do not like the look of today's school lunch. What is polite and sensible? | Try a little and leave the rest politely | Push the plate away and say it is disgusting | Throw it in the bin straight away without tasting any | Swap it for someone else's lunch secretly
exercise: You forgot your packed lunch at home. What do you do? | Tell the teacher or office so they can help | Go without eating anything all day and say nothing | Take food from someone's lunchbox | Walk home to get it
exercise: You knock over your juice in the lunch hall. What do you do? | Tell a lunch supervisor and help clean it up | Walk away quickly before anyone sees it | Cover it with a napkin and leave it there for the cleaners | Blame the child next to you
exercise: You have an apple core, a juice carton and a crisp packet. What do you do with them? | Put each in the right bin | Leave them on the table | Put them all in the recycling | Push them under the bench
exercise: A classmate has no lunch today. What is the kindest way to help? | Tell a teacher quietly so the school can help | Announce it so everyone shares | Ask him loudly why he has no food | Laugh about it with friends
exercise: On a trip to the zoo you are given a buddy. What do you do? | Stay with your buddy all day | Stay with your buddy until lunch | Swap buddies whenever you like | Go off alone if your buddy is too slow
exercise: You start to feel sick on the coach. What helps? | Tell the teacher early and look out at the horizon | Read a book to take your mind off it | Sit at the back and say nothing | Eat a big bag of sweets
exercise: In a museum you see an old Roman helmet with no sign about touching. What do you do? | Look but do not touch | Touch it gently with one finger | Pick it up to feel how heavy it is | Knock on it to hear the sound
exercise: You stroked the lambs on a farm trip. What must you do before eating your picnic? | Wash your hands well with soap and water | Wipe your hands on your trousers | Nothing, as lambs are clean animals and carry no germs at all | Just blow on your hands
exercise: You have $20 for a trip to the aquarium. What is a wise plan? | Decide before you go how much to spend | Spend it all in the first shop you find | Keep it loose in your coat pocket so it is easy to reach | Lend it to your friends on the bus so they can buy treats
exercise: You are going on a three-night school camp. What helps? | Pack with a parent and label your things | Pack alone at the very last minute | Take nothing with your name on it | Bring all your favourite toys so you will not miss home
exercise: On the first night of camp you feel homesick. What is best? | Talk to a teacher; the feeling usually passes | Hide it and cry alone all night | Phone home and ask to be fetched at once | Pretend to be ill so you can leave
exercise: You lose sight of your buddy in a busy science museum. What do you do? | Tell the teacher straight away | Keep exploring on your own until you find them | Wait for your buddy outside the museum | Go back to the coach and wait alone
test: A girl in Ohio has a peanut butter cookie in her bag, and her school is nut-free. Why should she leave it at home? | A trace of nut could harm an allergic classmate | Cookies are not allowed at lunch | Peanut butter is bad for her teeth | The teachers want to keep the cookies for themselves at break
test: Why should you tell a supervisor about a spilled drink on the lunch hall floor? | Someone could slip on the wet floor | The supervisor will want to drink it | Spills make the food go cold | The floor might change colour
test: On a farm trip in Kent, a boy feeds a goat and then eats his crisps without washing. What could happen? | Germs such as E. coli could make him ill | Nothing, as goats carry no germs on their mouths or fur | The goat will follow him home | His crisps will taste of goat
test: Your friend keeps saying she is not hungry and never has lunch. What is the best thing to do? | Quietly tell a teacher so the school can help | Tell everyone in your class so they can share | Ignore it, because it is not your business | Ask her parents yourself at the gate in front of everyone
test: A boy in Sydney spends all his trip money in the first gift shop and has nothing left for the ice cream. What could have helped? | Deciding before the trip how much to spend | Bringing no money at all | Borrowing from the teacher | Buying everything in the last shop instead
test: Why do teachers use a buddy system on trips? | Nobody can get lost without someone noticing | Buddies carry each other's bags | It makes the coach quieter | Buddies must share their lunch
test: In an art gallery, a sign says "Please do not touch". Why does the gallery ask this? | Hands can damage precious exhibits | The paintings are all still wet and sticky | Touching sets off the fire alarm in the gallery | The artist might be watching from the corner
test: You feel homesick at camp on the first night. What is true? | It is normal and usually passes quickly | It means you should never go on camp again | Only babies and very young children feel homesick | It will last the whole trip and get worse each night
test: You really dislike the fish pie served at school every Friday. What could you do? | Talk to your parents about a packed lunch on Fridays | Throw the pie on the floor | Refuse to eat anything at all on Fridays | Tell the cooks loudly that their food is horrible and you won't eat it
test: On the coach to a museum in Quebec, a girl begins to feel queasy (a bit sick). What should she do first? | Tell the teacher early | Keep it secret until she is sick | Read her book closely | Walk up and down the bus
test: forgot my lunchbox at home, starving | Tell the teacher or the office | Take a friend's lunch | Go hungry and say nothing | Walk home to get it
test: spilt my juice all over the floor oops | Tell a lunch supervisor and help clean it | Walk away quickly | Cover it with napkins and leave | Wait and see if someone slips on it, that's funny
test: on the coach trip, feeling queasy | Tell the teacher early and look at the horizon | Read your book to forget about it | Eat lots of sweets, sugar helps | Say nothing at all till you arrive, it'll pass
test: farm trip, just stroked the goats, lunch now yay | Wash your hands well first | Wipe them on your jeans and eat | Eat with one hand only | Lick your fingers clean
explain: A boy in New York called Sam, who has a nut allergy, swapped lunches with a friend; the cookie had peanuts, his face and throat swelled, and the school nurse had to use his EpiPen (a pen-shaped injection for a dangerous allergic reaction) and call 911.
explain: On a school trip in England a girl wandered away from her group to look at a shop; she got lost in the crowd, and the teachers spent an hour searching with the police.
fact: For a child with a serious food allergy, even a small bite of the wrong food can be life-threatening.
fact: Children who leave their group on trips get lost quickly in crowds, so you stay with your group and your buddy.
test: swap ur sandwich for my cookie? (he's got a nut allergy) | Don't swap, the wrong food can make him very ill | Fine if it's only a small bite of the edge | Fine if he picks the nuts out | Only if the cookie looks nut-free from the outside
test: on the trip, wanna pop into that shop, back in 2 mins | Stay with your group and your buddy | Go quick, nobody will notice | Go if a friend comes | Go and meet them at the bus
test: Why did the school nurse use an EpiPen when Sam's face swelled at lunch? | It was a dangerous allergic reaction | He had caught a very bad cold that morning | He had eaten too fast | He was far too hot in the crowded lunchroom
test: did u hear a kid shared his peanut cookie and his mate with the allergy got rushed to hospital? real lesson? | Even a small bite can be life-threatening; don't share | A small bite of nut is always fine | Only whole peanuts are dangerous, not cookies with bits in | Allergies only matter for adults
test: apparently on the zoo trip a kid wandered off and they found him 2 hours later in the gift shop. what's the true lesson? | Stay with your buddy; tell a teacher if separated | Go to the gift shop and wait there if you get lost | Wander off only if you know the way | Trips are too dangerous to go on at all
test: my mate says that boy never has lunch cos his family's poor, everyone knows. pass it on? | No, tell a teacher quietly; don't spread it | Yes, so everyone shares with him | Yes, it's not a secret | Yes, if you say it kindly and don't laugh
exam: Identify the reason for which many schools are nut-free. | Tiny traces of nut may harm a child with severe allergy | Nuts are too costly for school meals | Nuts make classrooms untidy | Nuts are forbidden by all religions and by the law of every country
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response to travel sickness on a school trip? | Tell the teacher early, sit near the front, look at the horizon | Read a book to take one's mind off it | Look at a phone screen | Sit at the very back of the coach and say nothing until arrival
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon spilling a drink in the lunchroom. | One tells a supervisor and helps clean it | One leaves it for others to notice | One covers it with a napkin and leaves it for the cleaning staff | One blames the nearest pupil
exam: Ascertain the course of action for a pupil separated from his group on a trip. | He informs the teacher or a member of staff without delay | He goes home by himself on public transport | He follows a stranger out | He hides until found
